{"name":"Orangery","hex":"#e6bca9","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"orangery","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A greenhouse in which orange trees are grown.","A garden or plantation where orange trees are grown."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/orangery"}],"entries":[{"title":"Orangery","blocks":[{"text":"An orangery or orangerie is a room or dedicated building, historically where orange and other non-hardy fruit trees are protected during the winter, as a large form of greenhouse or conservatory. In the modern day an orangery could refer to either a conservatory or greenhouse built to house fruit trees, or a conservatory or greenhouse meant for another purpose."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/County_Cork_-_Fota_House-Orangery_-_20180910130154.jpg/330px-County_Cork_-_Fota_House-Orangery_-_20180910130154.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orangery","wikidata":"Q952885"},{"title":"Orangery (Royal Garden of Prague Castle)","blocks":[{"text":"The Orangery, more specifically the New Orangery, is a modern greenhouse in the Royal Garden of Prague Castle, Czechia. It was built between 1999 and 2001 on the site of the former renaissance orangery. It is located next to the Ball Game Hall, on the edge of the Deer Moat. It was designed by Czech architect Eva Jiřičná in high-tech architecture style for Olga Havlová, wife of former Czech president Václav Havel. It is used for growing tropical flowers and plants which are used in Prague Castle. The construction is 100 meters long."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/Oran%C5%BEerie_v_Kr%C3%A1lovsk%C3%A9_zahrad%C4%9B.jpg/330px-Oran%C5%BEerie_v_Kr%C3%A1lovsk%C3%A9_zahrad%C4%9B.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orangery_(Royal_Garden_of_Prague_Castle)","wikidata":"